1
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:05,120
Hello again!

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,340
In this lecture we are going to discuss the
Bernoulli distribution.

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:15,420
Before we begin, we use “Bern” to define
a Bernoulli distribution, followed by the

4
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:19,360
probability of our preferred outcome in parenthesis.

5
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:24,570
Therefore, we read the following statement
as “Variable “X” follows a Bernoulli

6
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:30,280
distribution with a probability of success
equal to “p””.

7
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:31,980
Okay!

8
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:36,560
We need to describe what types of events follow
a Bernoulli distribution.

9
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:43,269
Any event where we have only 1 trial and two
possible outcomes follows such a distribution.

10
00:00:43,269 --> 00:00:48,159
These may include a coin flip, a single True
or False quiz question, or deciding whether

11
00:00:48,159 --> 00:00:52,200
to vote for the Democratic or Republican parties
in the US elections.

12
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,699
Usually, when dealing with a Bernoulli Distribution,
we either have the probabilities of either

13
00:00:57,699 --> 00:01:03,589
event occurring, or have past data indicating
some experimental probability.

14
00:01:03,589 --> 00:01:07,610
In either case, the graph of a Bernoulli distribution
is simple.

15
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:13,100
It consists of 2 bars, one for each of the
possible outcomes.

16
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:18,670
One bar would rise up to its associated probability
of “p”, and the other one would only reach

17
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:21,050
“1 minus p”.

18
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:26,040
For Bernoulli Distributions we often have
to assign which outcome is 0, and which outcome

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,640
is 1.

20
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:30,930
After doing so, we can calculate the expected
value.

21
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:37,070
Have in mind that depending on how we assign
the 0 and the 1, our expected value will be

22
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:41,460
equal to either “p” or “1 minus p”.

23
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:46,030
We usually denote the higher probability with
“p”, and the lower one with “1 minus

24
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:47,190
p”.

25
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:52,530
Furthermore, conventionally we also assign
a value of 1 to the event with probability

26
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:55,330
equal to “p”.

27
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:59,460
That way, the expected value expresses the
likelihood of the favoured event.

28
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:05,600
Since we only have 1 trial and a favoured
event, we expect that outcome to occur.

29
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:10,539
By plugging in “p” and “1 minus p”
into the variance formula, we get that the

30
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:17,700
variance of Bernoulli events would always
equal “p, times 1 minus p”.

31
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:21,120
That is true, regardless of what the expected
value is.

32
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:26,709
Here’s the first instance where we observe
how elegant the characteristics of some distributions

33
00:02:26,709 --> 00:02:29,400
are.

34
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:33,460
Once again, we can calculate the variance
and standard deviation using the formulas

35
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:36,720
we defined earlier, but they bring us little
value.

36
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:40,920
For example, consider flipping an unfair coin.

37
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:45,599
This coin is called “unfair” because its
weight is spread disproportionately, and it

38
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:49,140
gets tails 60% of the time.

39
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:54,110
We assign the outcome of tails to be 1, and
p to equal 0.6.

40
00:02:54,110 --> 00:02:58,520
Therefore, the expected value would be “p”,
or 0.6.

41
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:04,660
If we plug in this result into the variance
formula, we would get a variance of 0.6, times

42
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:09,850
0.4, or 0.24.

43
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:21,490
Great job, everybody!

